A||||10||Groot||Timing belt / valves||||5||
Z||000000||Groot||09-28-1999||10:33 AM||||Hi,<P>i have a 84 2.5L 4 cylinder with 118.000 miles. Should i worry about the timing belt<BR>or chain ? By the way is it a belt or chain ?<P>How often does this break ?<P>Thanks for responding,<BR>Egbert||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-28-1999||09:07 PM||||yes you should worry. replace them if at all posible. that's allot of miles on the factory 84 setup.<P>it's a gear set. no belt. no chain.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Groot||09-29-1999||09:06 AM||||Hi,<P>But if it is a gear set ... it could<BR>become less accurate by worn but <BR>it's not likely to stop working ??<BR>Or ... practice prooves otherwise ?<P>Egbert||||reg||5
Z||000003||theogre||09-29-1999||09:17 AM||||I take it you've never seen the OEM cam gears. They are Nylon Teeth over an Aluminum body. This is true of both chain driven, and gear driven OEM cams. <P>The most common failure mode is sudden failure of the nylon teeth. I do mean sudden. On sec it will run fine. next it's not moving an inch.<P>In flat tappet setups the cam load is considerably higher than roller lifter setups....... Average life for these gears has been running 110-130K miles in the pre roller 4 cyl. It's probably around the same in the V6.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TOM||09-29-1999||12:14 PM||||Wait. You mean the V6 has that cheap nylon gear running with a chain?||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||09-29-1999||11:34 PM||||As far as I know. Yes. Nearly ever stock timing set I've ever seen has had nylon cam gear teeth. Even my 289 ford had it. they stopped making the 289 around 68-69. Even replacment sets often have them... <P>There are some design advantages to the composite gears. Like the fact they can absorb a fair amount of vibration. The disadvantage of course is that they do break. You'd be suprised how strong a good set is tho. It can actually be stronger in the long run than an all steel set. You can also get tighter tolerance gears that don't wear as fast as steel sets do at the same tolerance.<P>Like all gear/chain set tho, they do age. the composite gear does get brittle over time. even more so in neglected motors. It will eventually break.... even in roller lifter setups which have signifigantly reduced cam load.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||09-29-1999||11:38 PM||||Cheap? Not really. the extra steps in making a nylon gear make it close to the same cost as CNC cutting a steel one.||||reg||1
Z||000007||SloPonti||09-30-1999||12:15 AM||||the fiero motor i am rebuilding now is a 4 banger and it had the all metal timing gears and the replacement gear is the nylon but the my factor gear was metal-why are some metal and some nylon-which is better?||||reg||1
Z||000008||TOM||09-30-1999||12:22 AM||||I better replace it then. I have 170k on it||||reg||1
Z||000009||Groot||10-01-1999||06:12 AM||||What happens if the nylon teeth fall apart ?<BR>Just stopping the engine or will all the<BR>pistons hit the valves ???||||reg||4
Z||000010||theogre||10-01-1999||08:46 PM||||in the 4, if you strip the gear it just don't go. I think it's the same on the 6. I think. Most stock motors are ok when the gear set quits... but then there is the notable exception of the Ford Escort.....<P>Neither is wholely better or worse by itself. It all depends on engine design, how often oil is changed, and how the engine is used.<P>A one peice gear is strong but can be prone to tooth wear caused by chattering. As the gear wears it chatters in the free space. A nylon gear can absorb more chatter impact, but also becomes brittle. Brittleness is partly caused by age and heat, and partly by the oil and contaminants in the motor. Modern oils are formulated with conditioners to preserve plastic parts, but these conditioners do break down over time.||||reg||1
